Crohn’s disease (CD) involves multifactorial genetic and immune dysregulation:
1) **NOD2 mutations** (e.g., rs2066844, rs2066845) impair bacterial recognition (MDP sensing), reducing NF-κB activation and antimicrobial peptide (e.g., α-defensins) secretion;
2) **ATG16L1** variants (e.g., T300A) disrupt autophagy, impairing bacterial clearance (e.g., *AIEC* persistence) and increasing Paneth cell dysfunction;
3) **IL23R** polymorphisms (e.g., rs11209026) enhance Th17 differentiation via STAT3 signaling, driving pro-inflammatory cytokine production (IL-17, IL-22);
4) **ER stress**: *XBP1* variants reduce Paneth cell function, exacerbating microbiome dysbiosis. Environmental triggers (e.g., smoking, diet) activate immune responses via NF-κB and NLRP3 inflammasome. Therapeutic strategies include **anti-TNF** (infliximab, UNITI trials), **anti-IL23p19** (risankizumab, ADVANCE trials), and **S1P modulators** (ozanimod, True North trial). *Summary:* CD pathogenesis involves *NOD2*, *ATG16L1*, *IL23R* dysfunction and autophagy defects, guiding cytokine-targeted therapies.*

